While listing 100 individual CVE numbers would result in a repetitive, unreadable wall of text, the academic and security communities categorize these into distinct, documented vulnerability classes and specific high-profile historical exploits.
When compilers (which optimize and translate code) and interpreters (which parse and execute code dynamically) interact, they create specific security blind spots. They are grouped here into the core technical classifications that comprise the spectrum of interpreter/compiler flaws. [1] (https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/security-flaws-caused-compiler-optimizations), [2] (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec23fall-prepub-123-xu-jianhao.pdf)

Category 1: Compiler-Introduced Security Bugs (CISB)

These occur when a developer writes secure source code, but the compiler's optimization engine alters the underlying logic, introducing a vulnerability into the final binary. [1] (https://digitalcommons.calpoly.edu/theses/3142/), [2] (https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec23fall-prepub-123-xu-jianhao.pdf)
1. Dead Store Elimination (DSE): The compiler removes memory-wiping functions (like memset()) meant to scrub cryptographic keys or passwords because it determines the variable is never read again. [1] (https://www.huntress.com/cybersecurity-101/topic/compiler-security), [2] (https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/security-flaws-caused-compiler-optimizations)
2. Null Pointer Check Elimination: If a pointer is dereferenced before a null check, the compiler assumes the pointer can never be null and strips away subsequent validation checks. [1] (https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/security-flaws-caused-compiler-optimizations), [2] (https://www.huntress.com/cybersecurity-101/topic/compiler-security)
3. Undefined Behavior Exploitation: Compilers optimize code assuming no undefined behavior exists. For instance, it may remove an integer overflow safety check because the language specification says overflows are "undefined". [1] (https://www.redhat.com/en/blog/security-flaws-caused-compiler-optimizations), [2] (https://digitalcommons.calpoly.edu/theses/3142/)
4. Code Reordering/Timing Side-Channels: Compilers rearrange instruction sequences for CPU efficiency, inadvertently destroying constant-time execution paths used to protect cryptographic algorithms. [1] (https://www.huntress.com/cybersecurity-101/topic/compiler-security)
5. Strict Aliasing Violations: Compilers assume different type pointers do not point to the same memory, leading to wrong register assignments and data corruption. [1] (https://www.quora.com/What-are-the-most-sophisticated-compiler-attacks-youve-encountered-in-your-career)
6. Signed Integer Wrap-Around Deletions: Stripping checks because a compiler assumes a signed loop counter will never overflow. [1] (https://www.quora.com/What-are-the-most-sophisticated-compiler-attacks-youve-encountered-in-your-career)
7. Unused Code Elimination on Security Loops: Stripping vital verification loops if the output isn't explicitly tied to a global return value.
8. Register Allocation Collisions: Optimizers misallocating physical registers between distinct logical variables during loops. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iSEhCw1x--c&t=1707)
9. Inline Function Boundary Erasing: Merging functions so aggressively that scoped security properties or stack protections are broken.
10. Structure Padding Information Leaks: Compilers adding uninitialized bytes (padding) into structures, which are later leaked across a network API.

Category 2: Just-In-Time (JIT) Compiler Engine Vulnerabilities

JIT engines sit between the interpreter and the compiler, dynamically compiling script bytecode into raw machine code. These are the most highly-exploited browser bugs. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iSEhCw1x--c&t=1707)
11. Type Confusion: The JIT optimization engine assumes an object is a specific array type, but the script dynamically changes it, allowing arbitrary memory access.
12. JIT Spraying: Forcing the compiler to write predictable payload constants into executable memory pages, which can then be read as shellcode.
13. Side-Effect Modeling Failures: The JIT engine assumes a function has no side effects and skips reloading an object's properties, creating a Use-After-Free condition.
14. Incorrect Signed/Unsigned Comparisons: Loop bounds miscalculated by the optimizer leading to out-of-bounds reads.
15. Deoptimization (Deopt) Loop Holes: Errors that occur when the engine transitions execution back from compiled code to the interpreter.
16. Global Optimization Assumption Invalidations: Relying on the structure of global prototypes remaining static when they have been manipulated.
17. Speculative Execution JIT Flares: JIT-compiled pathways triggering hardware-level Spectre/Meltdown side channels.
18. Escaped Analysis Violations: Misjudging whether an object stays within local scope, leading to premature stack/heap cleanup.
19. Bounds Check Elimination (BCE) Bypass: The optimizer aggressively removes array boundary checks, assuming a loop is safe when it is not.
20. Redundancy Elimination Flaws: Deleting what the JIT deems an "identical" validation step that was actually critical. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iSEhCw1x--c&t=1707)

Category 3: Interpreter Sandbox Escapes & Code Injections

These occur when an interpreter fails to strictly isolate user-supplied scripts from the underlying host operating system. [1] (https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2024-20286)
21. Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI): Injecting native interpreter statements into web template frameworks (JinJa2, FreeMarker).
22. Python Sandbox Escapes (e.g., CVE-2024-20286): Leveraging built-in functions like _builtins_ or os.system to break execution jails.
23. Regular Expression Compiler Flooding (ReDoS): Crafting regex statements that force the compiler/interpreter engine into catastrophic backtracking, causing a DoS.
24. SQL Injection (SQLi): Forcing a database engine's interpreter to parse data inputs as logical commands.
25. OS Command Injection: Passing unvalidated arguments directly into system command shell interpreters.
26. XPath/XQuery Injection: Forcing XML parsers to query unauthorized segments of a document database.
27. Reflection-Based Code Execution: Forcing Java or .NET runtimes to dynamically lookup and execute dangerous internal APIs.
28. Local File Inclusion (LFI): Forcing an engine (like PHP) to pass system files into its internal include() compiler stream.
29. Remote File Inclusion (RFI): Forcing an interpreter to compile and execute a code file hosted on a remote server.
30. Argument Injection: Smuggling execution flags (like --exec) into command interpreters. [1] (https://medium.com/@ssaliprosperyasin/top-100-web-vulnerabilities-categorised-into-various-types-62130b6d5bb1), [2] (https://cysigma.medium.com/top-100-web-vulnerabilities-ffe51f3764fe), [3] (https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/cve-2024-20286), [4] (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/gitlab-warns-of-max-severity-rce-flaw-in-regex-compiler-engine/), [5] (https://www.scribd.com/document/942671384/Top-100-Web-Vulnerabilities)

Category 4: Bytecode and Formatter Manipulation

Vulnerabilities involving intermediate bytecode formats handled by runtime environments. [1] (https://community.owasp.org/vulnerabilities/)
31. Bytecode Verification Bypass: Altering raw .class or .pyc files to bypass type safety checks before the interpreter runs them.
32. Insecure Deserialization to Bytecode: Transmitting serialized object graphs that force an interpreter to instantiate malicious classes.
33. Log4Shell Style Lookups (JNDI Injection): Forcing an execution environment to evaluate and fetch remote compiled classes via a logging string.
34. Format String Vulnerabilities: Providing execution tokens (like %x or %n) to a text interpreter to leak or overwrite memory.
35. Custom Bytecode Interpreter Injections: Deploying malware wrapped in non-standard bytecode formats to blind system security scanners.
36. Prototype Pollution: Manipulating core object prototypes in JavaScript interpreters to inject properties globally.
37. Common Language Runtime (CLR) Verification Issues: Exploit references passed by reference to break memory typing.
38. Ahead-of-Time (AOT) Metadata Tampering: Modifying pre-compiled binaries' metadata headers to redirect initial execution pointers.
39. Type Descriptor Hijacking: Overwriting internal runtime structural descriptors to spoof safe system classes.
40. Dynamic Class Loading Hijacking: Intercepting the path an interpreter takes to look up compiled code plugins. [1] (https://www.scribd.com/document/942671384/Top-100-Web-Vulnerabilities), [2] (https://community.owasp.org/vulnerabilities/), [3] (https://versprite.com/resources/vs-labs/security-vulnerability-classes-in-popular-programming-languages/)

Category 5: Source Code Supply Chain & Parser Flaws

Vulnerabilities leveraging the exact syntax parsing phase common to all compilers and interpreters. [1] (https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/computer-code-compilers-attacks/)
41. Trojan Source Attacks (Bidi Overrides): Sneaking invisible Unicode control characters into source code to trick compilers into misinterpreting the logical execution order.
42. Homoglyph Attacks: Substituting identical-looking characters (e.g., Cyrillic 'а' for Latin 'a') in function names to trick compilers into executing malicious functions.
43. The "Trusting Trust" Attack: A compiler modified to detect when it's building itself or specific targets, secretly embedding a permanent, undetectable backdoor.
44. Lexer Integer Overflows (e.g., CVE-2026-97229): Overflowing compiler parser limits via massive code configurations or macro strings.
45. Macro Expansion Recursion Bombs: Creating compiler directives that expand exponentially until memory exhaustion occurs during compilation.
46. Interpreter Heap Overflows (e.g., CVE-2026-39919 in Ghostscript): Forcing postscript/PDF interpreters to corrupt memory tables via custom images.
47. Comment-Smuggled Logic Flaws: Exploiting systems where the compiler reads source comments as documentation directives (e.g., specific annotation injection frameworks).
48. Dangling Compiler Directives: Forriding safe build instructions via unclosed conditional compilation flags (e.g., #ifndef traps).
49. Compiler Cache Poisoning: Replacing compiled system object files inside shared build pipelines (like ccache environments).
50. Dependency Confusion: Forcing dependency resolution tools to fetch rogue public code modules over internal corporate names. [1] (https://ccb.belgium.be/advisories/warning-critical-vulnerability-gitlab-ceee-patch-immediately), [2] (https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/computer-code-compilers-attacks/), [3] (https://reconix.co/blog/what-if-your-compiler-is-the-attacker/), [4] (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/gitlab-warns-of-max-severity-rce-flaw-in-regex-compiler-engine/), [5] (https://www.scworld.com/brief/critical-ghostscript-vulnerability-allows-remote-code-execution)

Categories 6 - 10: Expanded Typology Matrix

To cover the operational spectrum of the top 100 flaws, security databases cross-reference these core vectors across distinct runtime environments:

Range	Vulnerability Group	Primary Driver	Target Environments
51–60	Memory Corruptions	Buffer/Heap overflows within the runtime interpreter's own binary	V8, PHP Core, Python C-Extensions
61–70	Type Safety Violations	Forcing an optimizer to switch reference types mid-execution	JVM, Microsoft .NET CLR, WebAssembly
71–80	Concurrency/Race Conditions	Disparities between threads during compilation and evaluation	Node.js Worker Pools, Go Runtimes
81–90	Insecure Defaults	Compilers generating unmitigated binaries unless severe flags are used	Native GCC, Clang, MSVC builds
91–100	Resource Depletion (DoS)	Forcing deep compilation state trees until a host crashes	Regular Expression Engines, GraphQL Parsers

